I'd like to talk about a dish called shaking beef, which is one of my favorite Vietnamese foods.

It's a popular dish made from cubes of beef that are marinated and quickly stir-fried in a hot wok. As far as I know, it was influenced by French cuisine because beef became more widely consumed in Vietnam during the French colonial period.

If my memory serves me right, I first tried this dish at my cousin's wedding when I was a child. Although it was a long time ago, I still remember how impressed I was by its flavor. Since then, I've ordered it many times whenever I eat at Vietnamese restaurants.

What I love most about shaking beef is its combination of savory, slightly sweet, and smoky flavors. The beef is incredibly tender and juicy, and every bite is packed with seasoning. When it's served with fresh vegetables and a pepper-lime dipping sauce, the taste becomes even more refreshing and delicious.

Another reason I enjoy this dish is the memories associated with it. I often eat it when I'm spending time with my friends or family. Sitting together, chatting, enjoying the food, and sharing stories creates a warm and relaxing atmosphere.

Overall, shaking beef is not only a delicious dish but also one that brings back many happy memories. That's why it remains one of my all-time favorite foods.
